00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.490
My name is Philiana Ng,
Senior Editor of TV

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.724
at Entertainment Tonight.

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:26.493
We're going to be
digging into this

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.794
excellent British drama.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:31.331
That explores the
kind of increasingly

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.933
blurred lines
between what's real.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.604
And what's not.
In the show, ask

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:40.006
the question in this
kind of post truths era.

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.108
Can we really believe
what we see.

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.778
But before we dig into
this series, I'm gonna

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:48.281
introduce the panelists
who are spread out across

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.616
two continents.

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:53.453
First from the UK,
we have series creator,

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.922
writer and director
Ben Chanan.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:56.589
- Hello.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.692
- Executive producer, Rosie Alison

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:00.560
- Hello.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.294
- Holliday Grainger 

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:04.998
who plays
Detective Inspector Rachel Carey.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:05.832
- Hi, guys.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:09.369
- Callum Turner,
who plays Shaun Emery.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:10.003
- Hello.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.138
- And from the US we
have Ron Perlman

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.640
who plays Frank Napier.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.507
- Hey.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.576
- And Famke Janssen

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.812
who plays Jessica Mallory,
someone we meet

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.814
later in the series.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.015
- Hey everyone.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:22.549
Awesome.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.183
Hi guys.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:26.119
Thanks for hopping
on and doing this.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.188
Has this been--

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.923
Has it been awhile
since you've all

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:33.093
kind of seen each
other. In kind of,

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.529
one room or when
you know, how--

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:39.332
We've all been in lockdown.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.267
many, many months.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:41.868
I've seen it--

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:44.437
I think it was about
a year ago at a wrap party.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:48.007
Yeah, I like the one in
Gelson's actually.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:52.745
Yeah, but yeah, just
seen a lot of Callum

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:53.580
over the computer.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:54.814
Awesome.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.250
Before we get into the
Q&amp;A, we're going

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.853
to play the trailer
just so everyone

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.721
at home can kind of
get a better idea

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.123
of what the show is.

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:06.326
Ben do you want to
kind of tee up what

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.293
the show is about?

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.732
The show came about
through my work in documentaries, 

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.634
as I realized,
I worked with

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.736
the police quite a lot
in documentaries, 

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:21.040
and I realized that,
increasingly, people

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.176
are getting convicted
through video evidence

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.144
more and more reliably.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.447
And at the same time,
you can now get apps on

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:30.049
your phone that take,
you know, do digital

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.752
face transplants that
are cheap, effective,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.154
and convincing.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:36.289
I thought to myself,
you know, what if those

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.492
two technological and

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.427
criminological developments
were to collide.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.096
So that was the 
inspiration for it.

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:45.198
Cool.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:48.234
Yeah, let's play
the trailer.

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:51.638
In the last few hours, dramatic
footage has emerged online.

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:53.273
Depicting a man
believed to be former

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.743
soldier, Shaun Emery
attacking and abducting a woman.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.945
That's him,

00:02:58.945 --> 00:02:59.913
A woman's missing.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.251
And Shaun Emery is our suspect.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:10.790
Is anyone going to tell
me what the problem is,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.026
You're under suspicion
for assault and kidnapping.

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.829
You do know that your
suspect's a national hero?

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:19.432
Two tours in Afghanistan,
one in Iraq.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.000
And how do we
repay his service?

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.304
You mind telling
me where she is?

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:27.674
What happened between
you and Hannah Roberts?

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:28.474
She kissed me.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:29.242
She got on a bus.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:30.109
That's the last
I've seen her.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.445
This ain't what happened.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:33.112
This isn't what happened!

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.147
None of this happened!

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:35.048
This ain't real!

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.249
What have you done?!
This ain't real!

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:36.983
What have you done?!

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:41.721
What do you
mean he's gone?

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.656
He's escaped from
an emergency exit.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.258
Run facial rec,
make him a target.

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.763
Shawn claims this is
somehow being fabricated.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.433
Almost anything is
possible these days.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:54.634
If properly done,

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:55.602
it's impossible to prove.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.607
What are we tip-toeing around for?

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:02.275
I think someone's
tampering with evidence.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:03.910
We don't know whose
turf we're treading on.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:08.514
I understand you have this drive,

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:09.916
but counter-intelligence,

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.652
you can rarely see
the whole picture.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.320
I like when they say move along,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.021
nothing to see.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:16.789
They're lying.

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:20.460
The police think I've done it.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:21.294
My ex thinks I've done it.

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.829
and between me and
you, there are moments

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:23.896
when I'm scared that I die.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.100
If you knew what was at stake,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.468
you would walk away and pretend

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:29.402
this never happened.

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:32.405
The intelligence
community has hurt

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:33.172
a lot of people.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.208
I need to find
the soldier.

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:37.477
Find out what school
his daughter goes to.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.747
If she could see what we're working on,

00:04:40.747 --> 00:04:42.982
I'm talking about images,
that'll haunt you for

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:44.017
the rest of your life.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.854
While you're watching everybody else,
don't ever assume

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.555
there isn't somebody
watching me.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.273
Nice trailer.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:09.208
I mean I'm getting anxious, just

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.477
kind of like after that trailer.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.012
How are you guys feeling?

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.347
It's so good to see it again.

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:15.248
It's been so long

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:16.749
it's made me
feel really excited.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.785
It's pretty dynamic.

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:21.587
It's one of the--
it's very rare

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:25.391
to have a writer director
do the whole thing

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:28.561
So Ben has both written
all six episodes,

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:30.630
directed them all
and he just, he's a

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.864
master storyteller.

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.834
And he came up with this
extraordinary sort of

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.470
unmissable thriller,
where you really, it

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.139
makes you think it's
got the big picture.

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:43.376
The big picture
paranoia or about the

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:46.379
uncertainty of digital
manipulation, but he

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.248
also was very sure to
make you feel as well.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.284
And so he's got
these two fantastic

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:52.785
lead characters.

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:56.089
Uh, Shaun, the soldier.
Who's caught on

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:59.359
CCTV, apparently
committing a crime,

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:02.261
which he denies and
then Holliday, the

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:03.830
detective who begins.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.532
To investigate and
unravel the truth of

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.468
what really happened
and is our sort of,

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:11.037
eyes on the moral
maze of the story.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.573
But, uh, but it's all
credit to Ben so

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.942
I should stop talking,
but it's really rare

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:18.444
to find this master
storyteller who can do

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:21.247
all these things and
bring them together.

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:22.915
Yeah,

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:28.121
Thank you very much.

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:31.124
I mean, I certainly
didn't realize, 

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:32.992
how contemporary
it was going to be.

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.361
I started writing
this before the

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:36.362
term deep faith.

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:38.498
I I'd heard of it
before it became a.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:41.734
A common phrase,
even fake news.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.670
Wasn't really a
household phrase when

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.338
I started writing.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:47.974
And then as I was
writing, the sort of

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:50.376
the news started to
catch up with the story.

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.745
I started to factor
in the language

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.347
of the day into the,
into the dialogue.

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:55.982
Otherwise it would
have felt weird people

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:58.785
not referencing those
things, but clearly

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:02.989
we're living through a
time when every piece of

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:05.558
footage, every piece of
news is being questioned

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:06.392
and rightly so.

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:08.327
Uh, but then the flip
side of that is that

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.196
that's being exploited
by people as well.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:16.202
where it suits
certain politicians too.

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:18.404
If we don't quite
know what's real.

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:19.539
Yeah.

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:21.474
I mean, you kind of
mentioned the two

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:23.042
kind of amazingly
characters.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:27.246
Holliday, you play Rachel, who is 
this ambitious kind of eager?

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:29.482
Very skilled
detective.

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.684
Who's kind of thrown
into the mix in

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:35.087
this high profile 
Shaun Emery case.

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:35.455
Right.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.458
Uh, what stood out to
you about Rachel that

00:07:38.758 --> 00:07:40.359
kind of immediately
drew you in.

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:44.530
I kind of love
that when I first

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.133
started reading the
scripts, I didn't know

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:48.468
if I liked her or not.

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:51.404
And that it was, it
was a sort of, because

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:52.472
she doesn't really care.

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:53.940
It's like she's there
to do a job and then

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.741
she's ambitious and
she wants to do well

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:56.509
and prove herself.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:59.412
And, you know,
she's, she kind of, 

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:02.348
she's not necessarily
a people pleaser.

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:07.186
And I just love
Ben's, um, way that

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:11.023
Ben weaves such a moral
conundrum for Carey,

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:13.526
throughout the whole
series that she's, 

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:15.828
you know, when we first
met her, she has such

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:18.464
faith in the authorities
and the institution

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:19.098
of the police.

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:21.367
And, but she also has
like an incredibly

00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:24.136
strong moral compass
and a need to fight for.

00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:25.805
And get to the bottom
of the truth.

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:28.875
And when those two
aspects of her sort

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.878
of her faiths
like collide, 

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:34.413
it's a, this is kind
of complete yeah.

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:36.048
Moral maze that
she goes through.

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:38.117
Awesome.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.986
And Callum, I
mean, first of all,

00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:43.489
congratulations on
your BAFTA tv nomination.

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:46.492
Was that a
pleasant surprise?

00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:48.961
Yeah, it was a
lovely surprise.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:49.295
Yeah.

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:50.229
Yeah.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.200
What intrigued me
about Shaun was kind of,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.102
there was a point in
the series when even

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:57.904
he starts to question
his own understanding

00:08:57.904 --> 00:09:00.206
of the truth. As an
actor, how did you kind

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.274
of approach playing.

00:09:01.807 --> 00:09:03.776
That internal
struggle, like he's

00:09:03.776 --> 00:09:04.877
constantly on edge.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:07.480
Well, I was, I was
saw Shaun as like a

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.349
freedom fighter out of his 
own mind, in some

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:13.653
sense, because you
meet this man as he's

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.389
in the limelight, he's
famous for something

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:17.990
that no one wants to
really be famous for,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.259
for killing someone
and he's questioning

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:22.295
how he got there, how
he got to that place.

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:24.964
And he's really
trying to change.

00:09:25.431 --> 00:09:28.367
How the world views him,
how his family view him.

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:30.403
And how his friends
view him.

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:31.137
How he views himself.

00:09:31.571 --> 00:09:33.839
And he's, he's
about to do that.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.442
He gets off with the
case in the first

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:37.877
episode, and then
he's driving back into

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:41.781
this nightmare.
On a higher level.

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:43.916
It could be like a
long day's journey into

00:09:43.983 --> 00:09:46.218
the night kind of man,
searching for his soul

00:09:47.153 --> 00:09:48.654
journey that he goes on.

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:50.723
I just wanted
to do that.

00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:51.891
And then on top
of that, it was.

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:54.393
Working with Ben
and Holliday,

00:09:54.393 --> 00:09:56.696
and Rosie and those
guys in the BBC.

00:09:56.696 --> 00:09:59.298
And, uh, and the
character that was

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:00.199
really intelligent.

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.468
I think both the
characters that Holliday

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:04.503
and I have. Are working
out as we're going

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:06.973
along and alongside the audience.

00:10:06.973 --> 00:10:09.041
And I think that's
one of the reasons

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:10.509
that it's so gripping
because no one's ahead

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:11.677
of anyone, any stage.

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:13.613
Yeah, definitely.

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:16.349
Ron, I mean,
you've made a career

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:19.785
out of playing bad asses
I feel like, 

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:23.255
Like Hellboy and Clay
and Sons of Anarchy.

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:25.324
Frank kind of falls
under that umbrella

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:27.660
too, but there's a
limit to his power.

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:30.463
What was appealing
to you about Frank

00:10:30.463 --> 00:10:32.898
and the lengths that
he kind of goes to

00:10:33.499 --> 00:10:34.533
complete his missions?

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:38.371
Very similar to what
Holliday said, you

00:10:38.371 --> 00:10:41.540
know, first thing
you want to do is

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.776
figure out where you
fit into this story.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:48.280
And the thing that was
so smart about the way

00:10:48.314 --> 00:10:52.485
Ben rendered Frank, was
that, all the way

00:10:52.685 --> 00:10:54.153
to the end, you don't
know whether you're

00:10:54.153 --> 00:10:56.088
dealing with the good
guy or the bad guy.

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:56.555
You don't know.

00:10:56.589 --> 00:10:56.789
No.

00:10:56.789 --> 00:10:59.725
Whether you're supposed
to feel sympathy for

00:10:59.725 --> 00:11:02.328
for this guy's
plight or how he

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:05.031
views his place in
the world and his job.

00:11:05.831 --> 00:11:10.069
Or whether he's a
monster, um, and we'll

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:15.775
do anything in order to,
justify his agenda.

00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:21.213
His agenda seems
rather to be the tail

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:22.415
is wagging the dog.

00:11:23.015 --> 00:11:26.886
And so the
whole kind of thing

00:11:26.886 --> 00:11:28.521
